{'user_input': 'What is the recommended two-stage process for rotating the signing credentials used by GPU image build and promotion pipelines, including how to cut over safely and roll back if something goes wrong?', 'response': 'Based on the provided context, the recommended two-stage process for rotating signing credentials is a **two-phase operation: preflight (test) then swap (cutover) with automatic rollback on failure**.\n\nThe cutover and rollback details described are:\n- Update the alias `kms/gpu-image-signer` -> `key-vN` (atomic alias swap).\n- Revoke short-lived CI credentials associated with `key-v(N-1)` after a 5m grace, and rotate ephemeral role sessions.\n- Promote a canary image signed with `key-vN` and verify canary health for 20m across latency/error metrics.\n- Proceed to full fleet promotion if no triggers fired; otherwise, **revert the alias to `key-v(N-1)`** and mark `key-vN` as revocation-pending for forensic review.\n\nAn emergency rollback runbook (oncall) is also referenced.', 'reference': 'The recommended credential rotation uses a two-phase protocol: Phase A (Preflight, no live swap) and Phase B (Cutover).\nIn Phase A, provision a new KMS key version and point the signing alias (kms/gpu-image-signer) at the new version in a feature-flagged or test mode.\nIn Phase A, run test signings and confirm the attestation service accepts the new key version.\nIn Phase A, perform a shadow cross-account copy using the ephemeral CI role and the new signing metadata.\nIn Phase A, monitor for about 24 hours (or three release windows) for pull or auth regressions before cutting over.\nIn Phase B, schedule a change window and atomically swap the KMS alias to the new key version.\nAfter the Phase B swap, revoke short-lived CI credentials tied to the previous version after a 5-minute grace period and rotate ephemeral role sessions.\nIn Phase B, promote a canary image signed with the new key and watch canary health (latency and error) for about 20 minutes before full fleet promotion.\nIf issues occur, roll back by reverting the alias to the prior key version.\nAfter rollback, mark the new key version as revocation-pending for forensic review.', 'context_precision': 0.2916666666520833, 'context_recall': 0.7}
