{'user_input': 'What uptime and latency service level targets are stated for the hosted versus reserved/dedicated LLM inference offering in the SaaS go-to-market brief?', 'response': 'Hosted P95 for chat flows: 80–180ms (short prompts, <256 tokens). P99 target for Dedicated: <250ms. Additionally, pipeline endpoints inherit tenant availability SLAs (Hosted: 99.9%, Dedicated: 99.95).', 'reference': 'Hosted offering has a 99.9% regional uptime target.\nHosted offering typically delivers P95 latency of 80-180 ms for chat flows with short prompts under 256 tokens.\nDedicated or reserved offering has a contract-backed 99.95% uptime target.\nDedicated or reserved offering has a P99 latency target under 250 ms.', 'context_precision': 0.36666666664833336, 'context_recall': 1.0}
{'user_input': 'What is the procedure for an emergency rollback of a Serving Runtime release (Hosted and Dedicated)?', 'response': 'Based on the provided context, the emergency rollback procedure for a Serving Runtime release (Hosted and Dedicated) is as follows:\n\n**Rollback decision checklist (capture in the incident channel before executing):**\n- Current runtime release identifier (e.g., runtime-release-2025.02.01)\n- Intended rollback target (e.g., runtime-release-2025.01.17)\n- Blast radius (Hosted regions; Dedicated clusters/tenants)\n- Primary symptom (TTFT, throughput, 5xx, OOM)\n- Confirm the prior release is known-good (at least 24h stable in prod)\n\n**Step-by-step procedure:**\n\n1) **Freeze rollout / stop further progression**\n- If a rollout is in progress, pause it immediately (do not continue canary expansion).\n- Ensure no automated progressive delivery job will re-advance the rollout.\n\n2) **Hosted rollback (global fleets)**\n- Follow the CI/CD rollback doc to pin the Hosted runtime to the rollback target, then roll back in this order:\n  - non-critical region (if any still on canary only)\n  - remaining canary cohort\n  - all regions currently on the bad release\n- After each region completes, verify:\n  - 5xx rate normalizes\n  - GPU utilization returns to expected\n  - TTFT and token/s recover to baseline\n- Hosted verification queries (Console):\n  - Dashboard: "Hosted Inference / Runtime Release Health"\n  - Metrics to watch: request_error_rate, ttft_p95_ms, tokens_per_sec, gpu_oom_count\n\n3) **Dedicated rollback (tenant clusters)**\n- Dedicated rollbacks must consider customer-specific change windows and comms. In emergencies, proceed under the emergency change policy.\n- Identify impacted Dedicated clusters/tenants:\n  - Check "Dedicated / Runtime Release Adoption" dashboard\n  - Confirm whether each tenant is on the bad release\n- Execute rollback per cluster using the pipeline rollback procedure.\n- For high-sensitivity tenants, coordinate with CS for outbound comms and approvals (see comms guide).\n\n4) **Post-rollback validation**\n- Keep the incident open until metrics are stable for 30 minutes (Hosted) / 60 minutes (Dedicated)\n- Confirm request latency distributions return to baseline and no new error modes appear\n- Ensure autoscaling [continues per source]\n\n**Additional pipeline rollback details:**\n- Lock the tag (to prevent auto-advance) if the progressive delivery controller is configured to auto-advance:\n  - `rw-release lock --service serving-runtime --env hosted-prod --reason "Emergency rollback stability hold"`\n- Dedicated rollback per cluster:\n  - Step 1: Identify impacted clusters using the Console dashboard "Dedicated / Runtime Release Adoption" and confirm the cluster names (e.g., ded-use1-a, ded-euc1-b).\n  - Step 2: Pin tag per cluster: `rw-release set-tag --service [continues per source]`\n\n**Notes:**\n- Avoid rapid flip-flopping between releases; if you must re-roll forward, document the reason and require runtime lead approval.\n- Required artifacts for later review (capture links in the incident channel): CI/CD job link(s) for rollback, affected regions/clusters, metrics screenshots or dashboard permalinks, any customer comms sent.', 'reference': 'In an emergency rollback of a Serving Runtime release, first pause or freeze any in-flight progressive rollout so it cannot auto-advance.\nRe-pin the serving runtime to a known-good prior runtime release tag.\nTrigger a redeploy after re-pinning the runtime tag.\nVerify fleet health after the rollback using metrics such as 5xx rate, p95 TTFT, and GPU OOMs.\nClose the incident or change record only after verification is complete.\nFor Hosted, apply the rollback across regions in order: non-critical or canary first, then remaining canary, then all affected regions.\nFor Hosted, lock the runtime tag to prevent re-advance after rollback.\nFor Dedicated, identify the impacted tenant clusters.\nFor Dedicated, pin and redeploy per impacted tenant cluster.\nFor Dedicated, coordinate customer communications as required during the rollback.\nKeep the incident open until metrics are stable, approximately 30 minutes for Hosted and approximately 60 minutes for Dedicated.\nCapture incident evidence including CI/CD job links, affected regions or clusters, runtime tags, timings, and verification evidence.', 'context_precision': 0.8106150793549467, 'context_recall': 1.0}
{'user_input': "What is Redwood Inference's end-to-end process for rotating secrets and credentials used by production services (including emergency rotation)?", 'response': 'Based on the provided context, Redwood Inference\'s production secret rotation process is:\n\n**Preconditions:** Identify the secret name/path and all consumers (via the Credential Dependencies page), confirm rotation type (planned vs emergency), and ensure appropriate access (normal approvals or break-glass for emergencies).\n\n**Rotation patterns:** Pattern A is a "Dual-valid window" (preferred).\n\n**Emergency rotation during an incident:**\n- **Decision to rotate immediately** if any of the following are true: credible evidence of secret exposure; abnormal access patterns consistent with key misuse; vendor reports compromised credentials; attacker has access to systems that can read secrets. If impact is purely availability (no compromise signal), prefer standard mitigation first, as emergency rotation adds risk.\n- **Roles:** Incident Commander (authorizes emergency rotation and prioritizes which secrets first), Ops/SRE (executes changes and monitors service health), Security (advises on scope and evidence capture), Service owners (update consumers and validate functionality).\n- **Procedure:** Declare in the incident channel: "Emergency secret rotation in progress" listing candidate secrets (names/paths only) with the break-glass event (if used); open an incident and declare emergency rotation; use break-glass if required and never paste secret values; rotate highest-risk secrets first (Tier 0 then Tier 1); for widely shared credentials consider temporarily disabling the credential before cutover if safe; after containment, enumerate all downstream consumers and rotate any derived tokens; confirm consumers from the Credential Dependencies inventory and choose rotation pattern (dual-valid if available; cutover).\n\n**Post-rotation verification:** Confirm consumer rollout completion (all consuming services restarted/redeployed if required; for hot-reload services confirm at least one reload interval has passed; confirm there are no straggler consumers).\n\n**Documentation:** Planned rotation requires a change record with impacted services and steps.\n\nNote: The context does not provide the full end-to-end detail of each rotation pattern or all documentation requirements, so only the above can be confirmed.', 'reference': 'For planned rotation, the owning service team identifies the secret (Vault path) and all consumers via the Credential Dependencies inventory.\nFor planned rotation, the owning service team confirms tier and cadence and access before rotating.\nPreferred planned rotation method is dual-valid overlap: generate a new credential, write it as a new Vault version without deleting the old, deploy/update consumers to read the latest and if applicable accept both old and new, monitor authentication and service health for at least 30 minutes (at least 60 minutes for Tier 0), then disable or deprecate the old credential at the issuer, and remove the old Vault version after the overlap window (default 7 days).\nIf overlap is not possible, planned rotation uses a cutover: schedule a change window, change the credential at the issuer, immediately update Vault, restart or redeploy all consumers, and validate with rollback if needed.\nAfter any rotation, the team completes a verification checklist that includes ensuring all rollouts/reloads completed, monitoring auth/dependency metrics, running critical canaries, confirming audit logs, and revoking any break-glass access.\nAfter any rotation, the rotation event is recorded in a change record.\nFor emergency rotation (suspected compromise, exposure, or anomalies), an incident is opened and emergency secret rotation is declared using secret names/paths only.\nDuring emergency rotation, break-glass access may be used via the approved workflow for time-limited (60 minute) Tier 0/1 rotate access with full audit logging.\nDuring emergency rotation, IC/Security/SRE prioritize rotating Tier 0 then Tier 1.\nDuring emergency rotation, they use dual-valid overlap where possible or cutover otherwise, update the issuer and Vault, and restart/redeploy all consumers.\nDuring emergency rotation, they pause to reassess if impact worsens and record exact timestamps.\nAfter emergency rotation, break-glass access is revoked immediately and the rotated secrets/services and any follow-up rotations (derived/cached tokens) are documented in the incident timeline and post-incident summary for Security review.', 'context_precision': 0.9093537414836079, 'context_recall': 0.3333333333333333}
